10
00:00:43,807 --> 00:00:46,676
NARRATOR: We reveal buried secrets.

11
00:00:46,776 --> 00:00:49,612
MAN: I've just been informed that they discovered something here.

12
00:00:49,712 --> 00:00:52,182
Oh, look at this.

13
00:00:52,282 --> 00:00:57,187
WARD: (Laughter) A sphinx!

14
00:00:57,287 --> 00:01:02,492
NARRATOR: With discoveries that could rewrite ancient history.

15
00:01:02,592 --> 00:01:05,161
Phew, (laughs)

16
00:01:06,662 --> 00:01:13,136
This time, Egyptologists are searching for the secrets of the afterlife.

17
00:01:13,236 --> 00:01:18,475
Alejandro's team discovers a 4,000-year-old sealed burial chamber.

18
00:01:18,575 --> 00:01:20,877
WOMAN: Oh, that's incredible.

19
00:01:20,977 --> 00:01:22,412
ALEXANDER: Bingo!

20
00:01:22,512 --> 00:01:26,516
NARRATOR: John and Colleen decode an ancient guide to the underworld.

21
00:01:26,616 --> 00:01:29,920
COLEEN: The texts, the scenes and the hieroglyphics make this tomb

22
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:31,888
in a resurrection machine.

23
00:01:31,988 --> 00:01:34,791
NARRATOR: And Susanne makes an extraordinary discovery.

24
00:01:34,891 --> 00:01:38,962
DR SUZANNE: Someone put their fist in to get into the cavity.

25
00:01:53,510 --> 00:01:59,115
NARRATOR: Ancient Egypt is one of the most sophisticated societies in history.

26
00:01:59,215 --> 00:02:07,790
They built the pyramids, colossal temples, driven by an obsession: they dedicated

27
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:12,629
their lives to prepare for death.

28
00:02:12,729 --> 00:02:16,432
For centuries, archaeologists have tried to decode

29
00:02:16,532 --> 00:02:20,269
the rituals and techniques that the ancient Egyptians

30
00:02:20,369 --> 00:02:23,907
used to enter the afterlife.

31
00:02:29,745 --> 00:02:35,251
Dr. Colleen and Professor John Darnell head to the Valley of the Kings.

32
00:02:35,351 --> 00:02:40,056
DARNELL: We are interested in studying and understanding the past and bringing it into the present,

33
00:02:40,156 --> 00:02:42,925
bring it back to life.

34
00:02:43,025 --> 00:02:49,098
To date, there is no substitute for fieldwork.

35
00:02:49,198 --> 00:02:55,638
NARRATOR: For 250 years, the Valley has captivated generations of archaeologists.

36
00:02:55,738 --> 00:02:59,008
The 19th century was a very productive time.

37
00:02:59,108 --> 00:03:02,812
They discovered more than 40 graves, each one revealing more clues.

38
00:03:02,912 --> 00:03:06,115
about the afterlife of ancient Egypt.

39
00:03:06,215 --> 00:03:08,952
COLLEEN: The sky is so blue today.

40
00:03:09,052 --> 00:03:14,257
NARRATOR: In 1817, an explorer made an astonishing discovery:

41
00:03:14,357 --> 00:03:16,759
the tomb of King Seti I.

42
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:18,761
COLLEEN: Good morning. DARNELL: Good morning.

43
00:03:18,861 --> 00:03:21,197
Good morning.

44
00:03:21,297 --> 00:03:24,100
COLLEEN: To understand the afterlife of ancient Egypt,

45
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:27,237
you must study the hieroglyphics and the scenes.

46
00:03:27,337 --> 00:03:33,376
The tomb of Seti I is perfect for understanding the foundations of beliefs

47
00:03:33,476 --> 00:03:37,413
Egyptians about the afterlife.

48
00:03:37,513 --> 00:03:43,987
NARRATOR: Beneath the Valley of the Kings, Seti's tomb measures 137 meters

49
00:03:44,087 --> 00:03:49,726
deep into the mountain.

50
00:03:49,826 --> 00:03:55,898
It is the deepest grave in the Valley.

51
00:03:55,998 --> 00:04:03,072
Ten chambers and seven ornamental corridors were decorated with elaborate

52
00:04:03,172 --> 00:04:05,475
scenes on each wall.

53
00:04:07,744 --> 00:04:14,050
These paintings combined with this immense tomb played a key role in the survival

54
00:04:14,150 --> 00:04:17,220
of the soul of the pharaoh after death.

55
00:04:29,098 --> 00:04:31,434
COLLEEN: Wow!

56
00:04:31,534 --> 00:04:38,007
It's amazing to see how well preserved these hieroglyphics are.

57
00:04:38,107 --> 00:04:43,279
NARRATOR: The discovery of Seti's tomb was decisive in Egyptology.

58
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:48,350
At that time experts were learning to decode hieroglyphics,

59
00:04:48,451 --> 00:04:51,987
and this tomb gave them a sea of secrets.

60
00:04:52,087 --> 00:04:59,496
They discovered that the structure of the tomb plays a role in guiding the pharaoh to the underworld.

61
00:04:59,596 --> 00:05:03,299
DARNELL: This descending passage is perhaps one of the elements

62
00:05:03,399 --> 00:05:04,967
most important of the tomb.

63
00:05:05,067 --> 00:05:11,941
We deciphered a text on the walls that shows and describes the inclined passage.

64
00:05:12,042 --> 00:05:17,847
that carries, that propels the deceased to the other world.

65
00:05:17,947 --> 00:05:21,518
COLLEEN: Seti I's tomb is so deep in the rock,

66
00:05:21,618 --> 00:05:26,522
which perfectly imitates the architecture of the underworld.

67
00:05:26,622 --> 00:05:31,761
NARRATOR: Seti I ruled for eleven years, expanding his empire by crushing

68
00:05:31,861 --> 00:05:36,098
to its rivals in the Middle East and Africa.

69
00:05:36,198 --> 00:05:40,769
He died suddenly in his thirties, but despite this,

70
00:05:40,869 --> 00:05:46,275
Seti's preparation for the afterlife was exceptional.

71
00:05:46,375 --> 00:05:51,414
He dedicated his fortune and power to building the most elaborate tomb in the Valley,

72
00:05:51,514 --> 00:05:54,083
and he did it quickly.

73
00:05:54,183 --> 00:05:58,187
COLLEEN: It's very important to note that they dug,

74
00:05:58,288 --> 00:06:04,594
They painted and decorated this entire tomb in 12 years.

75
00:06:04,694 --> 00:06:08,331
NARRATOR: The scribes wrote entire books on the walls,

76
00:06:08,431 --> 00:06:14,470
instructions that would help Seti traverse the underworld.

77
00:06:14,570 --> 00:06:22,778
COLLEEN: Calling this Great God by the Gods of the underworld.

78
00:06:22,878 --> 00:06:25,781
That is what we see represented: the sun,

79
00:06:25,882 --> 00:06:30,553
the Great God of whom the hieroglyph speaks, being transported.

80
00:06:30,653 --> 00:06:34,157
And the hieroglyphics are repeated here.

81
00:06:34,257 --> 00:06:36,125
"De du güa ti iu".

82
00:06:36,225 --> 00:06:40,663
The Gods of the underworld.

83
00:06:40,763 --> 00:06:49,204
NARRATOR: The images on these walls reveal Seti I's route through the afterlife.

84
00:06:49,304 --> 00:06:53,442
The ancient Egyptians believed that immortality was possible if one followed

85
00:06:53,542 --> 00:07:00,316
to the sun god, Ra, who was born in the east every day and died every night

86
00:07:00,416 --> 00:07:02,118
and entered the underworld.

87
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:07,557
If Seti's soul managed to successfully navigate the treacherous underworld

88
00:07:07,657 --> 00:07:15,064
Together with the Sun God, he too could be reborn every morning in an eternal cycle.

89
00:07:15,164 --> 00:07:17,567
This tomb contained detailed instructions

90
00:07:17,667 --> 00:07:22,372
and the resources necessary for that dangerous journey.

91
00:07:31,681 --> 00:07:36,586
COLLEEN: We have arguably one of the best examples of the transition

92
00:07:36,686 --> 00:07:39,755
to the beyond.

93
00:07:39,855 --> 00:07:43,159
NARRATOR: Hidden in the inscriptions, Colleen finds evidence

94
00:07:43,259 --> 00:07:47,630
that not only the pharaoh can make the journey to the underworld.

95
00:07:47,730 --> 00:07:54,570
COLLEEN: Here, the people of northeast Egypt, the Nubians of the south, and Libya to the west.

96
00:07:54,670 --> 00:07:59,041
Apparently, no matter where you came from, you could enjoy the same

97
00:07:59,141 --> 00:08:02,545
beyond like the Pharaoh of Egypt.

98
00:08:02,645 --> 00:08:06,249
NARRATOR: But to find out how Seti made the transition to the afterlife,

99
00:08:06,349 --> 00:08:10,019
The Darnells need to decode the inscriptions inside

100
00:08:10,119 --> 00:08:11,955
from the mortuary chamber.

101
00:08:16,292 --> 00:08:20,296
225 km south of the Valley of the Kings in Aswan,

102
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:29,138
A Spanish archaeological team is excavating another ancient necropolis.

103
00:08:30,405 --> 00:08:36,913
Qubbet el-Hawa was a burial ground for elite members of society.

104
00:08:37,013 --> 00:08:42,118
These 4,500-year-old tombs offer key clues about rituals

105
00:08:42,218 --> 00:08:47,123
funerary and how the ancient Egyptians prepared for the afterlife.

106
00:08:48,557 --> 00:08:52,394
This is the team's tenth season working here,

107
00:08:52,494 --> 00:08:55,398
but Egyptologist Martina Bardonová continues

108
00:08:55,498 --> 00:08:59,235
getting used to starting at 5 AM.

109
00:08:59,335 --> 00:09:05,108
DR MARTINA: I go to sleep at 12 or 1 AM, and now I have to change.

110
00:09:05,208 --> 00:09:10,146
It's a kind of work jet lag, it's a work thing.

111
00:09:10,246 --> 00:09:13,515
You're here because you love your job, that's all.

112
00:09:13,615 --> 00:09:14,817
You wake up.

113
00:09:23,993 --> 00:09:27,230
NARRATOR: This season, Martina focuses on a new grave.

114
00:09:31,433 --> 00:09:32,869
DR MARTINA: I work as a miner.

115
00:09:34,036 --> 00:09:37,340
NARRATOR: He hopes this corridor leads to a burial chamber.

116
00:09:37,440 --> 00:09:39,041
DR MARTINA: Now I take the top part.

117
00:09:39,141 --> 00:09:41,177
I want to dig to the bottom.

118
00:09:41,277 --> 00:09:44,780
I am considering what is the best way.

119
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:47,617
The wall is full of termites.

120
00:09:47,717 --> 00:09:52,254
So I continue digging halfway down the corridor.

121
00:09:52,354 --> 00:09:56,959
NARRATOR: Termites are only found near a food source, such as wood.

122
00:09:57,060 --> 00:10:02,632
These insects are a sign that there might be something behind the stone wall.

123
00:10:07,570 --> 00:10:12,341
Crossing the hill, Martina's mission director, Professor Alejandro Jiménez

124
00:10:12,441 --> 00:10:16,779
Serrano is resting.

125
00:10:16,879 --> 00:10:19,282
PROF ALEJANDRO: Perhaps this is the best time of day.

126
00:10:24,186 --> 00:10:25,821
Tell me, Luisa, what's going on?

127
00:10:25,921 --> 00:10:32,261
WOMAN: It seems Martina found an intact camera in its place.

128
00:10:32,361 --> 00:10:35,631
PROF ALEJANDRO: Bingo! Looks like we have another camera.

129
00:10:35,732 --> 00:10:39,535
untouched where Martina is digging. Uhhh.

130
00:10:42,005 --> 00:10:43,072
Let's go!

131
00:10:47,409 --> 00:10:51,580
NARRATOR: Egyptian tombs have been plundered by robbers for millennia,

132
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:56,352
Finding an intact tomb would be an exceptional discovery for the team.

133
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:00,289
PROF ALEJANDRO: How are you? -Yes, I can.

134
00:11:00,389 --> 00:11:03,626
Thanks. Tell me what's up?

135
00:11:03,726 --> 00:11:07,330
DR MARTINA: There are termites.

136
00:11:07,430 --> 00:11:10,433
PROF ALEJANDRO: I must step there. -If you can.

137
00:11:10,533 --> 00:11:12,835
Be careful.

138
00:11:12,935 --> 00:11:20,143
We have a wall made of stones with plaster, and it looks like it's sealing something.

139
00:11:20,243 --> 00:11:22,845
DR MARTINA: I suspected it, but I didn't tell you.

140
00:11:22,945 --> 00:11:25,480
Because I wasn't sure.

141
00:11:25,581 --> 00:11:28,016
NARRATOR: If this is an intact chamber,

142
00:11:28,117 --> 00:11:31,687
It could contain mummies and funerary offerings.

143
00:11:31,787 --> 00:11:39,128
PROF ALEJANDRO: No one in 4,000 years has seen what is behind this wall.

144
00:11:45,367 --> 00:11:49,671
NARRATOR: Alejandro faces a challenge, he can't risk collapsing

145
00:11:49,771 --> 00:11:55,811
the wall of the burial chamber as it could contain precious artifacts

146
00:11:55,911 --> 00:11:58,848
PROF ALEJANDRO: Tell José to bring the computer and the camera.

147
00:11:58,948 --> 00:11:59,916
NARRATOR: But he has a plan!

148
00:12:05,621 --> 00:12:08,691
PROF ALEJANDRO: We used this drill to inspect before

149
00:12:08,791 --> 00:12:13,896
to open any intact chamber because we don't know what's inside.

150
00:12:13,996 --> 00:12:17,467
NARRATOR: Consider that it measures 40 centimeters thick.

151
00:12:23,505 --> 00:12:25,942
PROF ALEJANDRO: It seems simple, but it is not.

152
00:12:28,877 --> 00:12:32,114
Ah, Jose.

153
00:12:32,214 --> 00:12:36,852
This is a small camera with light, I'm going to insert it to check if there is any

154
00:12:36,952 --> 00:12:40,690
information about what is behind the wall.

155
00:12:42,457 --> 00:12:48,997
-He's talking about something. -He's a bit slow.

156
00:12:49,098 --> 00:12:53,335
Hey, wait, look. That's rubble.

157
00:12:53,435 --> 00:12:56,305
There are no materials, there is no pottery.

158
00:12:56,405 --> 00:12:58,173
DR MARTINA: I think it's a hallway.

159
00:12:58,273 --> 00:13:03,712
PROF ALEJANDRO: Yes, I think we haven't reached the burial chamber yet.

160
00:13:03,813 --> 00:13:08,751
Archaeology is often frustrating because you don't find what you expected to find.

161
00:13:08,851 --> 00:13:12,287
But it's all part of the game.

162
00:13:12,387 --> 00:13:15,391
The real prize is finding the burial chamber.

163
00:13:15,491 --> 00:13:20,496
We'll have to clear all this material, and maybe find another wall.

164
00:13:20,596 --> 00:13:24,033
We have to work a little harder.

165
00:13:24,133 --> 00:13:28,303
NARRATOR: Alejandro can't see how far this camera extends,

166
00:13:28,403 --> 00:13:31,073
there could be a lot of debris to clear.

167
00:13:36,512 --> 00:13:41,783
In the Valley of the Kings, the Swiss Egyptologist, Professor Susanne Bickel,

168
00:13:41,884 --> 00:13:45,287
returns from his tenth season of excavation.

169
00:13:45,388 --> 00:13:52,962
Today the team reopened a tomb that was excavated in the 1970s, tomb number 61.

170
00:13:53,062 --> 00:13:57,733
PROF SUSANNE: There is a certain excitement, a certain anticipation.

171
00:13:57,833 --> 00:14:01,971
NARRATOR: Susanne hopes to find the grave's original owner.

172
00:14:02,071 --> 00:14:06,776
Could you tell us how the engineers carried the deceased to the underworld?

173
00:14:10,246 --> 00:14:15,017
PROF SUSANNE: Oh, this one's not very good.

174
00:14:15,117 --> 00:14:21,156
It's too small and unfinished.

175
00:14:21,256 --> 00:14:25,361
It is halfway through being prepared.

176
00:14:25,461 --> 00:14:28,397
Why didn't they finish it?

177
00:14:34,069 --> 00:14:37,907
As long as you don't touch it, it won't move on its own.

178
00:14:38,007 --> 00:14:44,747
But um, oops, he did it.

179
00:14:44,847 --> 00:14:48,784
I think going out would be a positive thing.

180
00:14:48,884 --> 00:14:51,287
The whole package will fall.

181
00:14:54,456 --> 00:14:57,493
How tragic.

182
00:14:57,593 --> 00:15:01,597
NARRATOR: Now the team must try to secure the tomb before continuing.

183
00:15:01,697 --> 00:15:04,000
with the excavations.

184
00:15:06,802 --> 00:15:11,707
Professor Bickel has excavated eleven graves throughout the Valley.

185
00:15:11,807 --> 00:15:16,177
In the laboratory they are documenting and analyzing their discoveries,

186
00:15:16,278 --> 00:15:18,681
including human remains.

187
00:15:18,781 --> 00:15:20,249
PROF SALIMA: Yes, excellent.

188
00:15:20,349 --> 00:15:22,184
Oh, this is fantastic.

189
00:15:22,284 --> 00:15:26,454
NARRATOR: Professor Salima Ikram of the American University in Cairo,

190
00:15:26,555 --> 00:15:29,258
She is an expert in mummification.

191
00:15:29,358 --> 00:15:31,960
He is investigating how the ancient Egyptians prepared

192
00:15:32,060 --> 00:15:35,697
for the afterlife through this technique.

193
00:15:35,797 --> 00:15:39,601
PROF SALIMA: There are certain things that we already know about the mummification of different

194
00:15:39,701 --> 00:15:42,571
periods, but of course it is a learning process.

195
00:15:46,808 --> 00:15:50,145
PROF SALIMA: And look here how it sinks.

196
00:15:50,245 --> 00:15:58,754
I think they wrapped the fingers separately and then placed a larger cloth on top,

197
00:15:58,854 --> 00:16:00,655
and then they wrapped it together.

198
00:16:00,756 --> 00:16:02,524
WOMAN: Yes.

199
00:16:02,624 --> 00:16:05,427
NARRATOR: These body parts are further evidence of how

200
00:16:05,527 --> 00:16:09,465
mummification techniques evolved over time.

201
00:16:09,565 --> 00:16:12,667
PROF SALIMA: Embalmers did not always follow strict rules.

202
00:16:12,767 --> 00:16:17,973
Because sometimes something was fashionable for a while, then it was obsolete, and then it came back.

203
00:16:18,073 --> 00:16:22,111
There are different styles of mummification.

204
00:16:22,211 --> 00:16:25,447
NARRATOR: The ancient Egyptians believed that after death,

205
00:16:25,547 --> 00:16:28,317
the soul needed to return to the body,

206
00:16:28,417 --> 00:16:31,987
but if the body decomposed it would be unrecognizable

207
00:16:32,087 --> 00:16:34,890
and the soul would be lost for all eternity.

208
00:16:34,990 --> 00:16:38,493
Therefore, they mummified the elite of Egypt.

209
00:16:38,593 --> 00:16:43,798
The embalmers ritually bathed the deceased and removed his internal organs.

210
00:16:43,899 --> 00:16:48,069
and they used salt to dry the corpse.

211
00:16:48,170 --> 00:16:53,175
They removed the brain through the nose, but left the heart in place,

212
00:16:53,275 --> 00:16:56,645
since they believed that the soul resided there.

213
00:16:56,745 --> 00:17:02,317
Finally, they wrapped the mummy in linen and coated it in resins to ensure

214
00:17:02,418 --> 00:17:05,287
that they would remain recognizable for all eternity.

215
00:17:08,490 --> 00:17:13,161
PROF SALIMA: This is an excellent example of mummification in which

216
00:17:13,262 --> 00:17:18,867
appreciate that this leg looks like the leg of a living person,

217
00:17:18,967 --> 00:17:25,240
and that's because they made cuts in the skin and stuffed it.

218
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:31,146
They made a statue of yourself so that after you died and were incorporeal,

219
00:17:31,246 --> 00:17:36,051
Your corpse would be hollow and sunken, and this would give you back your body and make you

220
00:17:36,151 --> 00:17:38,921
a more permanent being like a type of statue.

221
00:17:44,726 --> 00:17:51,366
NARRATOR: After a day of digging, grave 61 is free of debris.

222
00:17:51,467 --> 00:17:57,206
It is now safe for Egyptologist Elina to examine it closely.

223
00:17:57,306 --> 00:17:59,207
ELINA: I think they started and then realized

224
00:17:59,307 --> 00:18:03,378
that it was dangerous to put coffins inside.

225
00:18:03,478 --> 00:18:07,616
So they didn't, they left and looked for another place.

226
00:18:12,654 --> 00:18:16,792
NARRATOR: Graves were supposed to last an eternity in the afterlife.

227
00:18:16,892 --> 00:18:23,599
In some parts of the Valley, fine limestone was the perfect material,

228
00:18:23,699 --> 00:18:28,471
but thick limestone like this is unstable.

229
00:18:28,571 --> 00:18:31,706
The sophisticated ancient engineers who built the tombs

230
00:18:31,806 --> 00:18:37,146
They realized that this site was not suitable.

231
00:18:37,246 --> 00:18:42,117
After finishing the excavation of the grave, the Swiss team packs up and leaves.

232
00:18:42,217 --> 00:18:44,386
to another place in the Valley.

233
00:18:48,590 --> 00:18:53,896
In Aswan, Martina spent two days excavating this corridor,

234
00:18:53,996 --> 00:18:56,899
hoping to find an intact burial chamber.

235
00:19:01,737 --> 00:19:03,238
MAN: Should I dig down? DR MARTINA: No, no, no, no

236
00:19:03,338 --> 00:19:05,341
because it's small. It doesn't fit.

